---
title: Exercise - The Projection Machine
description: Learn concepts of Change of Basis and Diagonalization.
thumbnail: exc5_thumbnail.png
language: en
scene: EXC5/exc5.scene
---
headline: Setting up a scene

All the main components needed for the task are already in the workspace. All object are in the start position at (0,0,0).

task: Add to M sequence axisAngle and translation transformations. 

Don't forget about a fact that matrix multiplication is not commutative. First in sequence must be rotation matrix (axisAngle).

![TRS](matrix_M.png)

For better view and control collapse nodes. You can do it by click on three dots in the top left corner of transformation once.

![Collapse](collapse_nodes.png)

--
headline: Setting up a scene

task: Try to change values in those matrices.

The sequence node works like matrix multiplication, so you get a single transformation matrix at the output.

You can also change the matrix values using manipulators directly in the Scene View.

To do this, click on the matrix in the Workspace, and then in the Scene View you will see manipulators that you can use directly.

![Manipulators](manipulators.png)

--
headline: The Eigenvalue Operation

This S matrix is the diagonal matrix of Eigenvalues, where 1 signifies a preserved dimension (vectors on the plane) and 0 signifies an annihilated dimension (the normal vector).

![Scale](scale.png)

--
headline: The Inverse Transformation

Now we need the inverse of the plane's transformation to transform coordinates from the general basis to the Eigenbasis.

task: Connect the output of the Plane Transform (M) to the input of the Inversion node (M^-1).

![Inverse](connect_inv.png)

--
headline: Constructing the Projector

The full projection operator is formed by chaining these matrices. 

This structure is a Similarity Transformation (Diagonalization) that yields the final projection matrix.

task: Connect S and M^-1 outputs to a matrix multiplicator S * M^-1.

![S*M^-1](first_mult.png)

task: Connect a plane transformation and a matrix multiplicator (S * M^-1) outputs to a matrix multiplicator M * (S * M^-1).

![M*S*M^-1](second_mult.png)

The multiplication M * S * M^-1 transforms the simple projection (S) from the Eigenbasis back to the general basis.

--
headline: The Object Transformation

The projection operator is now complete. We must define the transformation of the object being projected.

task: Add to C sequence axisAngle and translation transformations. 

Matrix C defines the position of the Operand (the vector set) that the projection Operator will act upon.

--
headline: Applying the Projection

The final step is to combine the object's transformation (C) with the projection operator.

task: Connect the output of the Projector Operator M * (S * M^-1) and the Cube Transform C to the final multiplication node M * (S * M^-1) * C. Plug the output into the projection sequence.

![M*S*M^-1*C](third_mult.png)

The order is M * (S * M^-1) * C means the operation M * (S * M^-1) is applied to the object's transformed coordinates C.

--
headline: Play time 

The final transformation *works* because:
- M * (S * M^-1) creates Operator based on simple Eigenvalues (S) and Eigenvectors (M).

- *Multiplication by C* applies this operator to the correct starting vector set (cube position).

- The entire process is the application of a linear map defined by a Similarity Transformation to the vectors defined by the object's transformation (C).

task: Change position of cube and plane as you want. 

Now this math construction will behave as a projection simulator, have fun! 

